ave 4 sunny lines to cover columns 5-8 (4+3+2+1 = 10 points). That's similar to earlier case: 4 sunny lines covering 10 points. Could we cover 10 points with 4 sunny lines? Possibly if we can have some lines covering 3 points. In columns 5-8, points are:\n\n- Column5: (5,1),(5,2),(5,3),(5,4)\n- Column6: (6,1),(6,2),(6,3)\n- Column7: (7,1),(7,2)\n- Column8: (8,1)\n\nTotal 10 points.\n\nWe can try to find sunny lines covering 3 points each. For example, line slope 1 through (5,1),(6,2),(7,3) but (7,3) not in set (since column7 max b=2). So only (5,1),(6,2). That's 2 points.\n\nLine slope 1 through (5,2),(6,3),(7,4) not in set. So only (5,2),(6,3). So 2 points.\n\nLine slope 1 through (5,3),(6,4) not in set.\n\nLine slope 1 through (5,4),(6,5) not in set.\n\nLine slope -1 through (5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1) is diagonal c=9, not allowed (non-sunny). So can't use.\n\nLine slope -2 through (5,4),(6,2),(7,0) not in set. So (5,4),(6,2) are uncovered.\n\nLine slope -0.5 through (5,4),(7,3) not in set.\n\nLine slope 2 through (5,1),(6,3) slope = (3-1)/(6-5) = 2, allowed. So line L1: (5,1),(6,3). Also (7,5) not in set. So covers 2 points.\n\nLine slope 0.5 through (5,1),(7,2) slope = (2-1)/(7-5) = 0.5, allowed. So line L2: (5,1),(7,2). Also (9,3) not in set.\n\nLine slope -1/2 through (5,4),(7,3) not in set.\n\nLine slope -2 through (6,1),(7,3) not in set.\n\nLine slope -1/3 through (5,4),(8,3) not in set.\n\nLine slope 3 through (5,1),(6,4) not in set.\n\nThus seems each sunny line can cover at most 2 points in this region. So 4 sunny lines can cover at most 8 points, insufficient for 10 points. So k=4 impossible for n=8 with vertical lines for columns 1-4.\n\nMaybe we can use diagonal non-sunny lines to cover more points in columns 5-8, reducing uncovered points.\n\nLet's try a different approach: Use non-sunny lines that are diagonals covering many points across columns, leaving fewer points for sunny lines.\n\nFor n=8, we have 4 non-sunny lines. We could use diagonal c=9 (points (1,8),(2,7),(3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1)) covering 8 points. Then we could use vertical lines for columns 1,2,3 maybe. Let's see.\n\nTake non-sunny lines:\n\n- Diagonal c=9 (8 points)\n- Vertical x=1 (8 points) but (1,8) already covered by diagonal, overlapping.\n- Vertical x=2 (7 points) overlapping with diagonal at (2,7).\n- Vertical x=3 (6 points) overlapping at (3,6).\n\nWe need 4 non-sunny lines total. Let's pick diagonal c=9, vertical x=1, vertical x=2, vertical x=3. Let's compute distinct covered points.\n\nVertical x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,8). Diagonal c=9 covers (1,8),(2,7),(3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1). Vertical x=2 covers (2,1)-(2,7). Vertical x=3 covers (3,1)-(3,6).\n\nNow count distinct points covered:\n\n- Column 1: all 8 points covered.\n- Column 2: all 7 points covered.\n- Column 3: all 6 points covered.\n- Diagonal points: (4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1) are not covered by vertical lines (since x=4..8). So these 5 points are covered by diagonal.\n\nThus total covered distinct points = 8+7+6+5 = 26 points. Uncovered points = total 36 - 26 = 10 points.\n\nWhich points are uncovered? Let's list columns 4-8, excluding diagonal points.\n\nColumn 4: points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4) (since (4,5) covered). So 4 points.\n\nColumn 5: points (5,1),(5,2),(5,3) (since (5,4) covered). So 3 points.\n\nColumn 6: points (6,1),(6,2) (since (6,3) covered). So 2 points.\n\nColumn 7: point (7,1) (since (7,2) covered). So 1 point.\n\nColumn 8: none (since (8,1) covered). So total uncovered = 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. Same as before.\n\nThus we still have 10 points left for 4 sunny lines. As before, each sunny line can cover at most 2 points, so impossible.\n\nMaybe we can use diagonal lines for columns 4-8 as well. For example, use diagonal c=8 (points (1,7),(2,6),(3,5),(4,4),(5,3),(6,2),(7,1)). That's 7 points. But we only have 4 non-sunny lines total. We could use diagonal c=9, diagonal c=8, vertical x=1, vertical x=2. Let's try that.\n\nNon-sunny lines:\n\n- Diagona